1
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:09,460
Your Grace, I want to say--

2
00:02:09,470 --> 00:02:10,960
You will not speak.

3
00:02:13,630 --> 00:02:16,110
How do I know you are who you say you are?

4
00:02:16,420 --> 00:02:18,340
If only I were otherwise.

5
00:02:18,340 --> 00:02:21,220
If you are Tyrion Lannister, why shouldn't I kill you

6
00:02:21,220 --> 00:02:23,540
to pay your family back for what it did to mine?

7
00:02:23,540 --> 00:02:25,640
You want revenge against the Lannisters?

8
00:02:26,510 --> 00:02:30,280
I killed my mother Joanna Lannister on the day I was born.

9
00:02:30,390 --> 00:02:34,120
I killed my father Tywin Lannister with a bolt to the heart.

10
00:02:34,310 --> 00:02:36,730
I am the greatest Lannister killer of our time.

11
00:02:36,730 --> 00:02:38,920
So I should welcome you into my service

12
00:02:38,930 --> 00:02:41,580
because you murdered members of your own family?

13
00:02:42,570 --> 00:02:44,650
Into your service?

14
00:02:45,160 --> 00:02:47,820
Your Grace, we have only just met.

15
00:02:48,010 --> 00:02:50,570
It's too soon to know if you deserve my service.

16
00:02:50,570 --> 00:02:52,850
If you'd rather return to the fighting pits,

17
00:02:53,240 --> 00:02:54,730
just say the word.

18
00:02:57,820 --> 00:03:00,150
When I was a young man, I heard a story

19
00:03:00,150 --> 00:03:03,060
about a baby born during the worst storm in living memory.

20
00:03:03,570 --> 00:03:06,070
She had no wealth, no lands, no army,

21
00:03:06,070 --> 00:03:08,990
only a name and a handful of supporters,

22
00:03:08,990 --> 00:03:10,680
most of whom probably thought

23
00:03:10,680 --> 00:03:12,700
they could use that name to benefit themselves.

24
00:03:12,770 --> 00:03:15,330
They kept her alive, moving her from place to place,

25
00:03:15,330 --> 00:03:18,080
often hours ahead of the men who'd been sent to kill her.

26
00:03:18,360 --> 00:03:21,890
She was eventually sold off to some warlord on the edge of the world

27
00:03:21,890 --> 00:03:24,060
and that appeared to be that.

28
00:03:24,060 --> 00:03:25,920
And then a few years later,

29
00:03:25,920 --> 00:03:28,100
the most well-informed person I knew

30
00:03:28,380 --> 00:03:31,360
told me that this girl without wealth, lands, or armies

31
00:03:31,360 --> 00:03:34,600
had somehow acquired all three in a very short span of time,

32
00:03:34,600 --> 00:03:37,010
along with three dragons.

33
00:03:37,020 --> 00:03:41,120
He thought she was our best, last chance to build a better world.

34
00:03:43,330 --> 00:03:45,560
I thought you were worth meeting at the very least.

35
00:03:45,560 --> 00:03:47,080
And why are you worth meeting?

36
00:03:47,230 --> 00:03:49,250
Why should I spend my time listening to you?

37
00:03:49,250 --> 00:03:51,830
Because you cannot build a better world on your own.

38
00:03:52,180 --> 00:03:55,770
You have no one at your side who understands the land you want to rule.

39
00:03:55,890 --> 00:03:58,090
The strengths and weaknesses of the houses

40
00:03:58,090 --> 00:03:59,260
that will either join or oppose you.

41
00:03:59,260 --> 00:04:01,060
I will have a very large army

42
00:04:02,170 --> 00:04:03,580
and very large dragons.

43
00:04:03,580 --> 00:04:06,420
Killing and politics aren't always the same thing.

44
00:04:07,490 --> 00:04:10,520
When I served as Hand of the King, i did quite well with the latter

45
00:04:10,520 --> 00:04:11,790
considering the king in question

46
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:14,390
preferred torturing animals to leading his people.

47
00:04:15,460 --> 00:04:17,350
I could do an even better job

48
00:04:17,350 --> 00:04:19,690
advising a ruler worth the name.

49
00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:21,990
If that is indeed what you are.

50
00:04:22,310 --> 00:04:23,990
So you want to advise me?

51
00:04:26,700 --> 00:04:27,950
Very well.

52
00:04:29,830 --> 00:04:31,770
What would you have me do with him?

53
00:04:33,390 --> 00:04:36,450
I swore I would kill him if he ever returned.

54
00:04:37,340 --> 00:04:38,470
I know.

55
00:04:42,410 --> 00:04:46,130
Why should the people trust a queen who can't keep her promises?

56
00:04:49,310 --> 00:04:52,340
Whomever Ser Jorah was when he started informing on you,

57
00:04:52,340 --> 00:04:54,030
he is no longer that man.

58
00:04:55,280 --> 00:04:57,170
I can't remember ever seeing a sane man

59
00:04:57,170 --> 00:04:59,670
as devoted to anything as he is to serving you.

60
00:04:59,920 --> 00:05:02,460
He claims he would kill for you and die for you

61
00:05:02,460 --> 00:05:05,490
and nothing I ever witnessed gives me reason to doubt him.

62
00:05:09,930 --> 00:05:11,630
And yet he did betray you.

63
00:05:24,250 --> 00:05:27,040
Did he have an opportunity to confess his betrayal?

64
00:05:27,040 --> 00:05:28,160
Yes.

65
00:05:28,970 --> 00:05:30,760
Many opportunities.

66
00:05:31,610 --> 00:05:32,980
And did he?

67
00:05:33,610 --> 00:05:37,710
No, not until forced to do so.

68
00:05:43,020 --> 00:05:44,290
He worships you.

69
00:05:45,170 --> 00:05:46,950
He is in love with you, I think.

70
00:05:47,780 --> 00:05:49,970
But he did not trust you with the truth.

71
00:05:49,970 --> 00:05:51,570
An unpleasant truth to be sure,

72
00:05:51,570 --> 00:05:53,600
but one of great significance to you.

73
00:05:54,300 --> 00:05:57,030
He did not trust that you would be wise enough to forgive him.

74
00:05:58,380 --> 00:06:00,050
So I should kill him?

75
00:06:01,330 --> 00:06:03,620
A ruler who kills those devoted to her

76
00:06:03,620 --> 00:06:05,760
is not a ruler who inspires devotion.

77
00:06:06,400 --> 00:06:08,600
And you're going to need to inspire devotion,

78
00:06:08,600 --> 00:06:12,420
a lot of it, if you're ever going to rule across the Narrow Sea.

79
00:06:14,080 --> 00:06:17,200
But you cannot have him by your side when you do.

80
00:06:22,670 --> 00:06:24,880
Remove Ser Jorah from the city.

81
00:07:36,350 --> 00:07:37,660
Confess.

82
00:07:38,790 --> 00:07:39,920
My son.

83
00:07:41,880 --> 00:07:43,010
Let me speak to--

84
00:07:43,950 --> 00:07:45,170
Confess.

85
00:07:45,440 --> 00:07:46,810
I meant it.

86
00:07:47,730 --> 00:07:49,970
My face will be the last thing you see--

87
00:08:07,630 --> 00:08:11,120
My name is Lanna. I'm an orphan.

88
00:08:12,830 --> 00:08:14,650
When I was eight, I begged enough money

89
00:08:14,650 --> 00:08:16,680
to buy my first bucket of oysters.

90
00:08:16,840 --> 00:08:20,110
I sold that bucket and made enough money to buy two more.

91
00:08:23,340 --> 00:08:26,500
It took a while, but I finally saved enough

92
00:08:26,500 --> 00:08:28,280
to buy myself an oyster cart.

93
00:08:29,740 --> 00:08:30,960
Now every morning,

94
00:08:30,960 --> 00:08:32,850
I make my way down to the canals.

95
00:08:34,900 --> 00:08:37,950
I pass the fish mongers and the bakers.

96
00:08:43,050 --> 00:08:44,580
I usually see Lhara,

97
00:08:44,580 --> 00:08:46,980
my first customer of the day on her way home.

98
00:08:49,050 --> 00:08:52,970
Then I turn left onto Moonsinger Lane...

99
00:08:55,180 --> 00:08:57,750
turn left onto Ragman Lane

100
00:08:58,160 --> 00:09:00,000
where I do most of my trade.

101
00:09:00,110 --> 00:09:01,960
Lanna is very impressive.

102
00:09:02,070 --> 00:09:03,780
Very industrious.

103
00:09:04,490 --> 00:09:07,480
She will make a fine servant for the Many-Faced God.

104
00:09:07,830 --> 00:09:09,500
How will she serve him?

105
00:09:09,500 --> 00:09:12,310
She will no longer turn left onto Ragman Lane.

106
00:09:12,720 --> 00:09:15,420
She will turn right and go to Ragman Harbor.

107
00:09:15,430 --> 00:09:17,120
What will she do there?

108
00:09:17,300 --> 00:09:18,720
She will see.

109
00:09:19,950 --> 00:09:21,470
See what?

110
00:09:22,020 --> 00:09:24,090
How can a man tell a girl this?

111
00:09:24,600 --> 00:09:25,890
If he knew what she would see,

112
00:09:25,890 --> 00:09:27,750
there would be no reason to send her.

113
00:09:28,100 --> 00:09:31,080
Oysters, clams, and cockles!

114
00:09:48,350 --> 00:09:51,330
Oysters, clams, and cockles!

115
00:09:59,340 --> 00:10:02,200
Oysters, clams, and cockles!

116
00:10:02,910 --> 00:10:04,550
Your oysters fresh?

117
00:10:06,340 --> 00:10:07,660
Best in the city.

118
00:10:07,670 --> 00:10:09,750
You wouldn't lie to an old man, would you?

119
00:10:24,880 --> 00:10:28,380
Very nice. Give me four with vinegar.

120
00:10:31,860 --> 00:10:33,500
It's my 18th voyage.

121
00:10:33,720 --> 00:10:36,140
Returned from all of them without a scratch on me.

122
00:10:42,970 --> 00:10:44,090
Can't do it.

123
00:10:45,360 --> 00:10:47,490
Please, I've three children.

124
00:10:47,490 --> 00:10:48,780
Without me...

125
00:10:48,810 --> 00:10:50,660
Here, take it. You have to.

126
00:10:50,660 --> 00:10:52,150
I've three children.

127
00:10:52,330 --> 00:10:53,870
- What will they do? - Come on.

128
00:10:53,870 --> 00:10:55,760
They've nothing without me.

129
00:10:55,770 --> 00:10:57,220
Please, you have to!

130
00:10:57,220 --> 00:10:59,400
Please, you don't understand!

131
00:10:59,740 --> 00:11:00,880
Please!

132
00:11:07,600 --> 00:11:09,360
The man is a gambler.

133
00:11:10,650 --> 00:11:14,590
He wages that a sailor's ship will make it to its destination.

134
00:11:15,700 --> 00:11:17,990
It is a strange wager for the captain.

135
00:11:18,220 --> 00:11:20,620
He only wins if he loses his life.

136
00:11:23,060 --> 00:11:26,150
So why would a captain make the wager in the first place?

137
00:11:27,840 --> 00:11:30,130
A girl tells a man that she has seen.

138
00:11:33,070 --> 00:11:34,680
If the captain dies,

139
00:11:35,290 --> 00:11:37,670
the thin man pays his family a lot of money.

140
00:11:39,900 --> 00:11:42,840
But perhaps the gambler loses his bet

141
00:11:42,840 --> 00:11:45,130
and decides he does not have to pay after all.

142
00:11:45,440 --> 00:11:47,600
A destitute woman and her small child,

143
00:11:47,600 --> 00:11:50,860
what can they do to such a man if he keeps their money for himself?

144
00:11:53,370 --> 00:11:55,940
To whom can they turn for recourse?

145
00:12:02,160 --> 00:12:03,710
The Many-Faced God.

146
00:12:05,750 --> 00:12:07,970
You said you didn't know what I would see at the docks.

147
00:12:07,980 --> 00:12:10,990
A man had no idea what a girl would see and what she would not.

148
00:12:13,220 --> 00:12:15,830
A girl named Lanna will return to the docks.

149
00:12:16,280 --> 00:12:17,860
She will watch the gambler.

150
00:12:17,860 --> 00:12:19,400
She will come to know as much about him

151
00:12:19,400 --> 00:12:21,010
as she knows about herself.

152
00:12:21,070 --> 00:12:22,390
And then what?

153
00:12:23,330 --> 00:12:27,410
A gift for the thin man.

154
00:12:47,160 --> 00:12:48,500
She's not ready.

155
00:12:49,660 --> 00:12:52,100
Perhaps she is, perhaps she's not.

156
00:12:52,350 --> 00:12:53,820
And if she's not?

157
00:12:54,750 --> 00:12:57,400
It is all the same to the Many-Faced God.

158
00:13:20,740 --> 00:13:23,320
The trial will take place soon.

159
00:13:23,320 --> 00:13:26,930
The High Sparrow will be presenting a substantial case against you.

160
00:13:26,930 --> 00:13:28,040
The charges?

161
00:13:28,040 --> 00:13:30,690
Fornication, treason, incest,

162
00:13:30,690 --> 00:13:32,350
the murder of King Robert.

163
00:13:32,350 --> 00:13:33,470
All lies.

164
00:13:33,470 --> 00:13:34,820
Of course, Your Grace.

165
00:13:39,460 --> 00:13:43,370
My concern is that the Faith does not adhere

166
00:13:43,380 --> 00:13:46,950
to the same standards of proof as the Crown.

167
00:13:46,950 --> 00:13:48,830
I hope you'll excuse me for saying it,

168
00:13:48,830 --> 00:13:51,650
but belief is so often the death of reason.

169
00:13:51,650 --> 00:13:53,500
I wish you had said it sooner.

170
00:13:56,290 --> 00:13:57,900
Is there any word from Jaime?

171
00:13:58,190 --> 00:13:59,830
No, I'm afraid not.

172
00:14:06,490 --> 00:14:09,950
Your Grace, Grand Maester Pycelle

173
00:14:10,860 --> 00:14:14,370
has summoned your Uncle Kevan back from Casterly Rock

174
00:14:14,370 --> 00:14:16,070
to serve as Hand of the King.

175
00:14:16,070 --> 00:14:18,680
He now presides over the small council.

176
00:14:20,060 --> 00:14:22,340
Tell him I need to speak to him.

177
00:14:22,340 --> 00:14:24,660
I implored him to visit you, but he would not.

178
00:14:29,290 --> 00:14:31,440
What about my son, the king?

179
00:14:32,180 --> 00:14:34,510
If you can visit me, then surely the king...

180
00:14:34,520 --> 00:14:36,870
Your arrest and Queen Margaery's arrest,

181
00:14:36,870 --> 00:14:38,430
the king has not taken them well.

182
00:14:38,430 --> 00:14:39,900
He remains in his chambers.

183
00:14:39,900 --> 00:14:43,640
His servants often find his food in the hall left untouched.

184
00:14:43,650 --> 00:14:45,530
You need to talk to him.

185
00:14:45,530 --> 00:14:47,840
You need to talk to my son and tell him to come and see me,

186
00:14:47,840 --> 00:14:49,060
come and see his mother.

187
00:14:49,060 --> 00:14:51,720
He-- I tried, Your Grace.

188
00:14:52,020 --> 00:14:54,170
He wouldn't see me. He won't see anyone.

189
00:14:55,740 --> 00:14:57,270
I can't stay here.

190
00:14:58,640 --> 00:15:02,010
There is a way, Your Grace.

191
00:15:05,970 --> 00:15:07,300
A way out.

192
00:15:09,830 --> 00:15:10,990
Confess?

193
00:15:12,570 --> 00:15:14,070
To the High Sparrow?

194
00:15:14,070 --> 00:15:15,640
I won't.

195
00:15:16,540 --> 00:15:18,150
I made him.

196
00:15:19,500 --> 00:15:21,360
I rose him up from nothing.

197
00:15:21,360 --> 00:15:24,010
I will not kneel before some barefooted commoner

198
00:15:24,010 --> 00:15:25,600
and beg his forgiveness.

199
00:15:33,580 --> 00:15:34,860
Good-bye, my queen.

200
00:15:37,530 --> 00:15:39,230
The work continues.

201
00:16:15,150 --> 00:16:16,200
Why?

202
00:16:18,580 --> 00:16:20,010
Why, Theon?

203
00:16:20,100 --> 00:16:23,270
I'm not Theon. There is no Theon.

204
00:16:23,270 --> 00:16:24,820
- Reek. - Reek.

205
00:16:24,820 --> 00:16:26,870
Why did you tell him, Reek?

206
00:16:28,490 --> 00:16:29,980
I was helping you.

207
00:16:30,670 --> 00:16:32,190
You wanted to escape.

208
00:16:32,190 --> 00:16:33,800
There is no escape.

209
00:16:35,290 --> 00:16:36,520
Not ever.

210
00:16:37,590 --> 00:16:39,740
Theon Greyjoy tried to escape.

211
00:16:41,210 --> 00:16:42,640
The master knew.

212
00:16:43,920 --> 00:16:45,470
He knows everything.

213
00:16:46,690 --> 00:16:48,910
He hunted him and caught him

214
00:16:48,910 --> 00:16:50,460
and strapped him to a cross

215
00:16:51,440 --> 00:16:53,430
and cut away piece after piece

216
00:16:53,650 --> 00:16:55,840
until there was no Theon left.

217
00:17:00,310 --> 00:17:01,460
Good.

218
00:17:04,710 --> 00:17:08,300
If it weren't for you, I'd still have a family.

219
00:17:08,990 --> 00:17:12,530
If I could do what Ramsay did to you right here, right now,

220
00:17:12,580 --> 00:17:14,240
I would.

221
00:17:18,640 --> 00:17:20,290
I deserved everything.

222
00:17:22,700 --> 00:17:24,660
I deserve to be Reek.

223
00:17:26,070 --> 00:17:27,740
I did terrible things.

224
00:17:30,120 --> 00:17:31,410
Turned on Robb.

225
00:17:32,650 --> 00:17:34,140
Captured Winterfell.

226
00:17:35,790 --> 00:17:36,720
Killed those boys.

227
00:17:36,720 --> 00:17:37,870
They weren't "Those boys."

228
00:17:37,870 --> 00:17:40,330
They were Bran and Rickon. They were your brothers.

229
00:17:40,330 --> 00:17:41,940
You've known them since they were born.

230
00:17:41,940 --> 00:17:43,350
They weren't. They were only--

231
00:17:43,380 --> 00:17:44,760
Only what?

232
00:17:45,810 --> 00:17:47,010
- I can't. - Tell me.

233
00:17:47,010 --> 00:17:48,610
I can't, not unless the master says.

234
00:17:48,620 --> 00:17:50,210
- Tell me. They weren't what? - They weren't--

235
00:17:50,210 --> 00:17:52,630
Tell me why Bran and Rickon should be gone

236
00:17:52,630 --> 00:17:54,340
while you still breathe the air.

237
00:17:54,340 --> 00:17:55,880
Tell me to my face, Theon.

238
00:17:55,890 --> 00:17:57,460
Tell me that they weren't your brothers!

239
00:17:57,470 --> 00:17:58,920
They weren't Bran and Rickon!

240
00:18:01,310 --> 00:18:02,690
I couldn't find them.

241
00:18:10,250 --> 00:18:11,700
It was two farm boys.

242
00:18:16,230 --> 00:18:18,760
I killed them and burned them so no one would know.

243
00:18:23,690 --> 00:18:24,830
You didn't?

244
00:18:26,890 --> 00:18:28,710
Do you know where they went, Bran and Rickon?

245
00:18:28,720 --> 00:18:30,040
I can't talk to you anymore.

246
00:18:30,040 --> 00:18:30,970
Theon, you have to tell me.

247
00:18:30,970 --> 00:18:31,990
Do you have any idea where--

248
00:18:31,990 --> 00:18:33,800
Not Theon! Reek!

249
00:18:39,780 --> 00:18:42,840
Our scouts tell us he's got no more than 6,000 men.

250
00:18:43,220 --> 00:18:45,510
More than half of those are mounted, however.

251
00:18:45,510 --> 00:18:47,680
And how high do Stannis's horses jump?

252
00:18:48,460 --> 00:18:50,580
Our walls have been fully repaired.

253
00:18:50,830 --> 00:18:52,670
The gates have been reinforced.

254
00:18:52,980 --> 00:18:54,900
We have enough food for six months.

255
00:18:55,900 --> 00:18:58,940
We are more prepared for a siege than they could ever be.

256
00:18:59,970 --> 00:19:02,240
All we have to do is wait for them to freeze,

257
00:19:02,470 --> 00:19:05,170
starve, and mutiny.

258
00:19:08,450 --> 00:19:09,680
You disagree?

259
00:19:09,680 --> 00:19:11,830
Stannis isn't from the North.

260
00:19:11,920 --> 00:19:13,220
You are, Father.

261
00:19:14,090 --> 00:19:17,180
I think you're missing an opportunity to show the people of the North

262
00:19:17,180 --> 00:19:20,200
how House Bolton treats southern invaders.

263
00:19:20,200 --> 00:19:21,260
And what do you recommend?

264
00:19:21,260 --> 00:19:22,670
That we not sit and wait for Stannis

265
00:19:22,670 --> 00:19:24,930
to decide what sort of fight this is going to be.

266
00:19:24,930 --> 00:19:27,470
That we hit first and hit hard

267
00:19:27,470 --> 00:19:29,590
and leave a feast for the crows.

268
00:19:29,590 --> 00:19:32,960
A smart commander does not abandon a defensive advantage.

269
00:19:33,170 --> 00:19:35,080
As long as we stay behind these walls,

270
00:19:35,080 --> 00:19:36,240
they can't touch us.

271
00:19:36,330 --> 00:19:38,140
Not to mention that the snow is so deep,

272
00:19:38,140 --> 00:19:40,110
we couldn't get an army through to engage them

273
00:19:40,110 --> 00:19:41,440
even if we wanted to.

274
00:19:41,440 --> 00:19:42,830
I don't need an army.

275
00:19:43,940 --> 00:19:45,750
I need 20 good men.

276
00:19:54,340 --> 00:19:55,810
So, have you decided yet?

277
00:19:56,040 --> 00:19:58,310
Whether I'm worthy of your service?

278
00:20:00,720 --> 00:20:03,960
Have you decided yet whether you're going to have me killed?

279
00:20:03,960 --> 00:20:05,690
It's probably my safest option.

280
00:20:08,990 --> 00:20:10,810
I can see why you would think so.

281
00:20:11,450 --> 00:20:13,410
It's what your father would have done.

282
00:20:14,540 --> 00:20:16,240
And what would your father have done?

283
00:20:18,240 --> 00:20:19,520
My father,

284
00:20:19,990 --> 00:20:22,410
who publically sentenced me to death?

285
00:20:22,740 --> 00:20:25,710
I'd say his thoughts on having me killed were abundantly clear.

286
00:20:25,710 --> 00:20:27,110
Is that why you killed him?

287
00:20:30,480 --> 00:20:33,120
Someday, if you decide not to execute me,

288
00:20:33,740 --> 00:20:36,050
I'll tell you all about why I killed my father.

289
00:20:36,090 --> 00:20:38,280
And on that day, should it ever come,

290
00:20:39,500 --> 00:20:41,360
we'll need more wine than this.

291
00:20:43,950 --> 00:20:45,550
I know what my father was.

292
00:20:46,490 --> 00:20:47,690
What he did.

293
00:20:48,960 --> 00:20:51,050
I know the Mad King earned his name.

294
00:20:54,050 --> 00:20:56,550
So, here we sit.

295
00:20:57,140 --> 00:21:00,190
Two terrible children of two terrible fathers.

296
00:21:00,190 --> 00:21:01,380
I'm terrible?

297
00:21:01,770 --> 00:21:03,200
I've heard stories.

298
00:21:03,800 --> 00:21:07,260
Why did you travel to the far side of the world to meet someone terrible?

299
00:21:07,260 --> 00:21:09,280
To see if you were the right kind of terrible.

300
00:21:09,280 --> 00:21:10,230
Which kind is that?

301
00:21:10,230 --> 00:21:13,000
The kind that prevents your people from being even more so.

302
00:21:15,120 --> 00:21:17,270
Well, I did reopen the fighting pits.

303
00:21:17,270 --> 00:21:20,130
Under my rule, murder will once again become entertainment.

304
00:21:20,130 --> 00:21:21,670
Yes, that was wise.

305
00:21:21,940 --> 00:21:25,340
And you agreed to marry someone you loathe for the greater good.

306
00:21:25,560 --> 00:21:26,990
Very impressive.

307
00:21:27,990 --> 00:21:30,560
My own sister married someone she loathed as well,

308
00:21:30,920 --> 00:21:32,180
though not by choice and

309
00:21:32,250 --> 00:21:34,770
certainly not for the greater good, gods forbid.

310
00:21:35,290 --> 00:21:37,170
She ended up having him killed.

311
00:21:39,020 --> 00:21:41,240
Perhaps it won't come to that.

312
00:21:48,780 --> 00:21:51,920
It's not impossible that Varys was right about you after all.

313
00:21:51,920 --> 00:21:55,080
Varys? King Robert's spymaster?

314
00:21:55,080 --> 00:21:57,980
Yes, he's the one who convinced me to come find you.

315
00:21:58,670 --> 00:22:00,380
He was my travelling companion

316
00:22:00,380 --> 00:22:02,650
before Ser Jorah seized that role for himself.

317
00:22:02,650 --> 00:22:04,790
Jorah sent my secrets to Varys.

318
00:22:05,770 --> 00:22:09,560
For 20 years the Spider oversaw the campaign to find and kill me.

319
00:22:09,560 --> 00:22:11,790
He did what he had to do to survive.

320
00:22:11,930 --> 00:22:14,750
He did a lot of other things as well, things he didn't have to do.

321
00:22:15,120 --> 00:22:18,820
I suspect he's the main reason you weren't slaughtered in your crib.

322
00:22:19,890 --> 00:22:21,090
But you trust him?

323
00:22:21,090 --> 00:22:22,350
Yes, oddly.

324
00:22:22,380 --> 00:22:24,960
He may be the only person in the world I trust.

325
00:22:26,010 --> 00:22:27,540
Except my brother.

326
00:22:28,040 --> 00:22:30,060
The brother who killed my father?

327
00:22:30,560 --> 00:22:31,850
That's the one.

328
00:22:31,850 --> 00:22:33,470
Perhaps I will have you killed after all.

329
00:22:33,470 --> 00:22:35,420
Your queenly prerogative.

330
00:22:38,140 --> 00:22:39,640
I had given up on life

331
00:22:40,450 --> 00:22:43,740
until Varys convinced me you might be worth living for.

332
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:46,150
If you chop off my head, well,

333
00:22:46,220 --> 00:22:48,300
my final days were interesting.

334
00:22:54,790 --> 00:22:56,290
I'm not going to kill you.

335
00:22:58,840 --> 00:23:01,530
No? Banish me?

336
00:23:01,530 --> 00:23:02,680
No.

337
00:23:03,050 --> 00:23:04,880
So if I'm not going to be murdered

338
00:23:04,880 --> 00:23:06,300
and I'm not going to be banished--

339
00:23:06,300 --> 00:23:07,950
You're going to advise me.

340
00:23:09,350 --> 00:23:11,820
While you can still speak in complete sentences.

341
00:23:16,070 --> 00:23:17,240
Advise you on what?

342
00:23:17,240 --> 00:23:18,780
How to get what I want.

343
00:23:18,820 --> 00:23:20,730
The Iron Throne.

344
00:23:22,200 --> 00:23:24,550
Perhaps you should try wanting something else.

345
00:23:24,550 --> 00:23:27,050
If I want jokes, I'll get myself a proper fool.

346
00:23:27,140 --> 00:23:28,800
I'm not entirely joking.

347
00:23:29,240 --> 00:23:31,620
There's more to the world than Westeros after all.

348
00:23:31,870 --> 00:23:33,310
How many hundreds of thousands of lives

349
00:23:33,310 --> 00:23:35,120
have you changed for the better here?

350
00:23:36,130 --> 00:23:37,910
Perhaps this is where you belong,

351
00:23:37,910 --> 00:23:39,740
where you can do the most good.

352
00:23:42,230 --> 00:23:44,970
I fought so that no child born into Slaver's Bay

353
00:23:44,970 --> 00:23:47,940
would ever know what it meant to be bought or sold.

354
00:23:48,030 --> 00:23:50,810
I will continue that fight here and beyond.

355
00:23:51,760 --> 00:23:53,380
But this is not my home.

356
00:23:53,550 --> 00:23:56,260
When you get back to your home, who supports you?

357
00:23:56,260 --> 00:23:57,600
The common people.

358
00:23:57,670 --> 00:24:00,290
Let's be generous and assume that's going to happen.

359
00:24:00,350 --> 00:24:02,760
Here in Slaver's Bay, you had the support of the common people

360
00:24:02,760 --> 00:24:04,640
and only the common people.

361
00:24:05,320 --> 00:24:08,800
What was that like? Ruling without the rich?

362
00:24:09,410 --> 00:24:11,510
House Targaryen is gone.

363
00:24:11,640 --> 00:24:14,740
Not a single person who shares your blood is alive to support you.

364
00:24:14,880 --> 00:24:16,490
The Starks are gone as well.

365
00:24:16,530 --> 00:24:18,490
Our two terrible fathers saw to that.

366
00:24:18,490 --> 00:24:20,010
The remaining members of House Lannister

367
00:24:20,010 --> 00:24:22,020
will never back you, not ever.

368
00:24:22,370 --> 00:24:24,370
Stannis Baratheon won't back you, either.

369
00:24:24,720 --> 00:24:25,740
His entire claim to the throne

370
00:24:25,740 --> 00:24:27,950
rests on the illegitimacy of yours.

371
00:24:28,120 --> 00:24:30,090
That leaves the Tyrells.

372
00:24:30,090 --> 00:24:32,780
Not impossible, not enough.

373
00:24:32,780 --> 00:24:34,120
Lannister, Targaryen,

374
00:24:34,120 --> 00:24:36,260
Baratheon, Stark, Tyrell.

375
00:24:37,220 --> 00:24:39,070
They're all just spokes on a wheel.

376
00:24:39,120 --> 00:24:41,480
This one's on top, then that one's on top.

377
00:24:41,480 --> 00:24:43,360
And on and on it spins,

378
00:24:43,360 --> 00:24:45,320
crushing those on the ground.

379
00:24:45,780 --> 00:24:48,780
It's a beautiful dream, stopping the wheel.

380
00:24:49,750 --> 00:24:52,210
You're not the first person who's ever dreamt it.

381
00:24:53,190 --> 00:24:55,140
I'm not going to stop the wheel.

382
00:24:56,830 --> 00:24:58,880
I'm going to break the wheel.

383
00:25:21,590 --> 00:25:25,200
You said whoever wins will fight at the Great Pit in front of the queen.

384
00:25:25,740 --> 00:25:27,110
I won.

385
00:25:27,500 --> 00:25:29,700
- You struck me. - Have me flogged

386
00:25:29,700 --> 00:25:30,920
if it makes you happy.

387
00:25:30,920 --> 00:25:32,610
But I'm the best you've got.

388
00:25:34,420 --> 00:25:36,470
If I win at the Great Pit,

389
00:25:37,250 --> 00:25:39,080
how much can you sell me for?

390
00:25:41,780 --> 00:25:43,080
You're a free man.

391
00:25:43,530 --> 00:25:45,340
You could have gone anywhere.

392
00:25:45,750 --> 00:25:47,700
Why did you come back?

393
00:25:52,200 --> 00:25:53,600
Let me fight for her

394
00:25:54,120 --> 00:25:55,850
and I belong to you.

395
00:26:01,550 --> 00:26:02,710
Thirsty?

396
00:26:04,670 --> 00:26:05,920
Confess.

397
00:26:09,850 --> 00:26:13,010
I'll get out of here, you realize, before long.

398
00:26:13,010 --> 00:26:14,090
Confess.

399
00:26:14,100 --> 00:26:16,270
I can make you a wealthy woman.

400
00:26:17,960 --> 00:26:19,520
A lady of the court.

401
00:26:19,520 --> 00:26:20,830
Confess.

402
00:26:25,310 --> 00:26:26,420
Or I can make sure you die

403
00:26:26,420 --> 00:26:29,200
in the most hideous way imaginable.

404
00:26:29,200 --> 00:26:31,210
And all I do is sit here

405
00:26:31,260 --> 00:26:34,230
imagining hideous ways for you to die.

406
00:27:19,930 --> 00:27:21,230
Does it hurt?

407
00:27:23,240 --> 00:27:24,380
No.

408
00:27:25,520 --> 00:27:26,780
Good.

409
00:27:30,750 --> 00:27:31,930
How are you?

410
00:27:32,950 --> 00:27:36,200
I'm not the one who got punched and kicked in the face.

411
00:27:38,360 --> 00:27:39,740
You know what I mean.

412
00:27:42,310 --> 00:27:43,470
I'm good.

413
00:27:44,300 --> 00:27:47,030
- Are you scared? - A little.

414
00:27:47,400 --> 00:27:48,720
Me, too.

415
00:27:52,760 --> 00:27:54,480
- Who's there? - Olly.

416
00:27:57,010 --> 00:27:58,290
Come in.

417
00:28:01,260 --> 00:28:02,740
I heard what happened.

418
00:28:02,780 --> 00:28:05,140
Oh, not to worry.

419
00:28:05,750 --> 00:28:08,700
Men brawl from time to time. It's only natural.

420
00:28:11,730 --> 00:28:13,130
Figured you were hungry.

421
00:28:14,920 --> 00:28:16,370
Oh, you're a good lad.

422
00:28:18,670 --> 00:28:20,110
Wanted to ask you something

423
00:28:20,950 --> 00:28:22,410
if you've got the time.

424
00:28:22,750 --> 00:28:24,630
I need to check on baby Sam.

425
00:28:32,130 --> 00:28:35,330
Now then, how can I help?

426
00:28:36,470 --> 00:28:38,760
- It's about the Lord Commander. - Yes?

427
00:28:38,820 --> 00:28:41,890
He's going up to Hardhome to save all them wildlings.

428
00:28:43,940 --> 00:28:46,180
Wildlings killed my mom and dad.

429
00:28:47,170 --> 00:28:48,830
They killed everyone in my village.

430
00:28:48,830 --> 00:28:50,570
We've been fighting them for years.

431
00:28:50,570 --> 00:28:52,020
This wasn't a fight.

432
00:28:52,020 --> 00:28:53,400
We were farmers

433
00:28:54,090 --> 00:28:55,720
and they slaughtered us.

434
00:28:57,050 --> 00:28:58,530
So why is he saving them?

435
00:28:59,190 --> 00:29:00,740
Wildlings are people.

436
00:29:01,840 --> 00:29:04,700
Just like us, there are good ones and bad ones.

437
00:29:04,700 --> 00:29:07,760
The one he's travelling with, the one with the red beard,

438
00:29:08,290 --> 00:29:10,330
he led the raid on my village.

439
00:29:10,330 --> 00:29:12,520
- Oh, Olly. - How can he trust him?

440
00:29:13,840 --> 00:29:16,400
I've seen the army of the dead.

441
00:29:17,480 --> 00:29:19,870
I've seen the white walkers.

442
00:29:19,920 --> 00:29:22,310
And they're coming for us, for all the living.

443
00:29:24,870 --> 00:29:26,110
And when it's time,

444
00:29:27,870 --> 00:29:30,450
we'll need every last man we can find.

445
00:29:31,070 --> 00:29:33,720
But what if we let the wildlings through the gates

446
00:29:33,730 --> 00:29:35,830
and they cut our throats while we sleep?

447
00:29:35,830 --> 00:29:38,180
Jon's taking a risk, but he has to.

448
00:29:38,180 --> 00:29:40,550
We don't stand a chance otherwise.

449
00:29:41,510 --> 00:29:44,820
Sometimes a man has to make hard choices,

450
00:29:44,830 --> 00:29:46,700
choices that might look wrong to others,

451
00:29:46,710 --> 00:29:49,460
but you know are right in the long run.

452
00:29:49,810 --> 00:29:51,320
You believe that?

453
00:29:51,380 --> 00:29:53,160
With all my heart.

454
00:30:00,500 --> 00:30:01,840
Try not to worry, Olly.

455
00:30:02,130 --> 00:30:04,120
I've been worrying about Jon for years.

456
00:30:05,760 --> 00:30:07,360
He always comes back.

457
00:30:49,850 --> 00:30:51,120
Turn us about!

458
00:31:24,170 --> 00:31:25,660
You trust me, Jon Snow?

459
00:31:27,190 --> 00:31:28,630
Does that make me a fool?

460
00:31:29,250 --> 00:31:30,910
We're fools together now.

461
00:31:47,620 --> 00:31:48,810
Lord of Bones.

462
00:31:51,170 --> 00:31:52,490
Been a long time.

463
00:31:53,170 --> 00:31:56,070
Last time I saw you, the little crow was your prisoner.

464
00:31:56,080 --> 00:31:58,790
The other way around now. What happened?

465
00:31:59,090 --> 00:32:00,140
War.

466
00:32:00,220 --> 00:32:02,730
War? You call that a war?

467
00:32:03,040 --> 00:32:05,260
The greatest army the North has ever seen

468
00:32:05,270 --> 00:32:08,200
cut to pieces by some southern king.

469
00:32:08,400 --> 00:32:10,060
We should gather the elders,

470
00:32:10,630 --> 00:32:12,490
find somewhere quiet to talk.

471
00:32:12,490 --> 00:32:14,180
You don't give the orders here.

472
00:32:14,180 --> 00:32:16,170
I'm not giving an order.

473
00:32:18,110 --> 00:32:20,010
Why aren't you in chains?

474
00:32:20,950 --> 00:32:22,360
He's not my prisoner.

475
00:32:23,350 --> 00:32:24,390
No?

476
00:32:24,760 --> 00:32:25,920
What is he?

477
00:32:26,320 --> 00:32:27,610
We're allies.

478
00:32:30,060 --> 00:32:32,100
You fucking traitor.

479
00:32:35,380 --> 00:32:37,210
You fight for the crows now?

480
00:32:39,520 --> 00:32:41,740
I don't fight for the crows.

481
00:32:43,430 --> 00:32:46,790
We're not here to fight. We're here to talk.

482
00:32:46,980 --> 00:32:48,230
Is that right?

483
00:32:49,370 --> 00:32:52,520
You and the pretty crow do a lot of talking, Tormund?

484
00:32:53,030 --> 00:32:54,730
And when you're done talking,

485
00:32:54,930 --> 00:32:57,880
do you get down on your knees and suck his cock?

486
00:33:13,200 --> 00:33:16,360
Gather the elders and let's talk.

487
00:33:42,350 --> 00:33:43,780
My name's Jon Snow.

488
00:33:46,220 --> 00:33:48,270
I'm Lord Commander of the Night's Watch.

489
00:33:51,690 --> 00:33:52,910
We're not friends.

490
00:33:54,130 --> 00:33:55,650
We've never been friends.

491
00:33:57,220 --> 00:33:59,040
We won't become friends today.

492
00:34:00,850 --> 00:34:02,560
This isn't about friendship.

493
00:34:05,080 --> 00:34:06,780
This is about survival.

494
00:34:07,010 --> 00:34:08,950
This is about putting a 700-foot wall

495
00:34:08,960 --> 00:34:11,210
between you and what's out there.

496
00:34:11,210 --> 00:34:13,290
You built that wall to keep us out.

497
00:34:13,290 --> 00:34:16,400
Since when do the crows give two shits if we live?

498
00:34:16,400 --> 00:34:19,640
In normal times we wouldn't. But these aren't normal times.

499
00:34:19,670 --> 00:34:22,920
The white walkers don't care if a man's free folk or crow.

500
00:34:22,920 --> 00:34:25,210
We're all the same to them, meat for their army.

501
00:34:26,010 --> 00:34:28,690
But together we can beat them.

502
00:34:28,690 --> 00:34:30,360
Beat the white walkers?

503
00:34:30,520 --> 00:34:33,260
Good luck with that. Run from them, maybe.

504
00:34:43,230 --> 00:34:44,480
It's not a trick.

505
00:34:48,000 --> 00:34:50,930
It's a gift for those who join us.

506
00:34:55,980 --> 00:34:57,200
Dragonglass.

507
00:34:58,880 --> 00:35:01,980
A man of the Night's Watch used one of these daggers

508
00:35:01,980 --> 00:35:03,230
to kill a walker.

509
00:35:04,170 --> 00:35:06,030
- You saw this? - No.

510
00:35:06,500 --> 00:35:07,830
But I trust the man.

511
00:35:07,880 --> 00:35:10,470
There are old stories about dragonglass.

512
00:35:10,530 --> 00:35:14,210
There are old stories about ice spiders as big as hounds.

513
00:35:14,210 --> 00:35:16,580
And with the things we've seen, you don't believe them?

514
00:35:16,880 --> 00:35:19,260
Come with me and I'll share these weapons.

515
00:35:19,260 --> 00:35:20,650
Come with you where?

516
00:35:20,780 --> 00:35:22,540
There are good lands south of the Wall.

517
00:35:22,540 --> 00:35:24,420
The Night's Watch will let you through the tunnel

518
00:35:24,420 --> 00:35:27,240
and allow your people to farm those lands.

519
00:35:29,930 --> 00:35:31,430
I knew Mance Rayder.

520
00:35:32,320 --> 00:35:34,070
He never wanted a war with the Night's Watch.

521
00:35:34,070 --> 00:35:36,500
He wanted a new life for his people, for you.

522
00:35:36,730 --> 00:35:39,320
We're prepared to give you that new life.

523
00:35:39,850 --> 00:35:40,640
If?

524
00:35:40,640 --> 00:35:44,220
If you swear you'll join us when the real war begins.

525
00:35:47,230 --> 00:35:48,380
Where is Mance?

526
00:35:51,870 --> 00:35:53,630
- He died. - How?

527
00:35:59,590 --> 00:36:01,310
I put an arrow through his heart.

528
00:36:09,620 --> 00:36:11,220
I say we send the Lord Commander

529
00:36:11,220 --> 00:36:14,510
back to Castle Black with no eyes.

530
00:36:16,590 --> 00:36:19,020
Hey, none of you saw Mance die.

531
00:36:19,440 --> 00:36:20,510
I did.

532
00:36:21,010 --> 00:36:24,040
The southern king who broke our army, Stannis,

533
00:36:24,330 --> 00:36:27,110
wanted to burn him alive to send us a message.

534
00:36:27,160 --> 00:36:29,920
Jon Snow defied that cunt's orders.

535
00:36:31,290 --> 00:36:33,450
His arrow was mercy.

536
00:36:34,170 --> 00:36:35,710
What he did took courage.

537
00:36:35,710 --> 00:36:37,350
And that's what we need today,

538
00:36:37,390 --> 00:36:39,050
the courage to make peace

539
00:36:39,050 --> 00:36:41,740
with men we've been killing for generations.

540
00:36:42,530 --> 00:36:44,570
I lost my father, my uncle,

541
00:36:44,570 --> 00:36:47,060
and two brothers fighting the damn crows.

542
00:36:47,060 --> 00:36:49,120
I'm not asking you to forget your dead.

543
00:36:50,200 --> 00:36:51,790
I'll never forget mine.

544
00:36:54,060 --> 00:36:57,140
I lost 50 brothers the night that Mance attacked the Wall.

545
00:36:59,210 --> 00:37:00,510
But I'm asking you

546
00:37:01,820 --> 00:37:03,910
to think about your children now.

547
00:37:05,720 --> 00:37:07,140
They'll never have children of their own

548
00:37:07,140 --> 00:37:08,720
if we don't band together.

549
00:37:10,280 --> 00:37:11,940
The Long Night is coming

550
00:37:12,670 --> 00:37:14,660
and the dead come with it.

551
00:37:15,230 --> 00:37:17,050
No clan can stop them.

552
00:37:17,400 --> 00:37:19,060
The free folk can't stop them.

553
00:37:19,060 --> 00:37:20,450
The Night's Watch can't stop them.

554
00:37:20,450 --> 00:37:23,010
And all the southern kings can't stop them.

555
00:37:24,920 --> 00:37:27,790
Only together, all of us.

556
00:37:27,910 --> 00:37:29,320
And even then it may not be enough,

557
00:37:29,320 --> 00:37:31,590
but at least we'll give the fuckers a fight.

558
00:37:47,610 --> 00:37:49,870
You vouch for this man, Tormund?

559
00:37:52,940 --> 00:37:55,130
He's prettier than both my daughters,

560
00:37:55,970 --> 00:37:57,590
but he knows how to fight.

561
00:37:57,910 --> 00:38:00,370
He's young, but he knows how to lead.

562
00:38:01,820 --> 00:38:03,710
He didn't have to come to Hardhome.

563
00:38:04,300 --> 00:38:06,090
He came because he needs us

564
00:38:07,750 --> 00:38:09,350
and we need him.

565
00:38:13,710 --> 00:38:15,560
My ancestors would spit on me

566
00:38:15,560 --> 00:38:17,560
if I broke bread with a crow.

567
00:38:18,190 --> 00:38:20,590
So would mine, but fuck 'em, they're dead.

568
00:38:30,320 --> 00:38:32,290
I'll never trust a man in black.

569
00:38:36,590 --> 00:38:38,210
But I trust you, Tormund.

570
00:38:39,100 --> 00:38:40,960
If you say this is the way,

571
00:38:42,890 --> 00:38:44,060
we're with you.

572
00:38:48,040 --> 00:38:49,170
This is the way.

573
00:38:51,230 --> 00:38:52,510
I'm with Tormund.

574
00:38:53,130 --> 00:38:55,410
We stay here, we're dead men.

575
00:38:57,270 --> 00:39:00,580
At least with King Crow, there's a chance.

576
00:39:03,780 --> 00:39:06,010
Tormund.

577
00:39:08,800 --> 00:39:11,190
Keep that new life you want to give us.

578
00:39:14,940 --> 00:39:17,300
And keep your glass, King Crow.

579
00:39:19,870 --> 00:39:23,080
As soon as you get on his ships, they're gonna slit your throats

580
00:39:23,090 --> 00:39:26,060
and dump your bodies to the bottom of the Shivering Sea.

581
00:39:28,450 --> 00:39:29,780
That's our enemy.

582
00:39:31,660 --> 00:39:33,610
That has always been our enemy.

583
00:39:49,950 --> 00:39:51,700
I fucking hate Thenns.

584
00:40:11,510 --> 00:40:13,840
- Move it. - Be careful.

585
00:40:14,300 --> 00:40:16,160
- Move 'em along, now. - Cast-off time.

586
00:40:16,260 --> 00:40:17,610
That's it, come on.

587
00:40:21,110 --> 00:40:22,820
How many are with us? 5,000?

588
00:40:23,870 --> 00:40:25,330
I'm not good at counting.

589
00:40:26,720 --> 00:40:28,220
We're leaving too many behind.

590
00:40:28,220 --> 00:40:29,770
The free folk are stubborn.

591
00:40:29,770 --> 00:40:32,260
You know how long it took Mance to band them together?

592
00:40:32,560 --> 00:40:34,690
20 fucking years.

593
00:40:35,020 --> 00:40:37,040
And he knew them better than I ever will.

594
00:40:38,420 --> 00:40:41,910
They're running out of food and there's nothing to hunt.

595
00:40:42,420 --> 00:40:43,620
They'll come around.

596
00:40:46,980 --> 00:40:49,180
- Get yourselves ready. - Aye!

597
00:40:49,440 --> 00:40:51,100
Johnna is gonna look after you.

598
00:40:51,100 --> 00:40:53,890
She's in charge. You listen to her.

599
00:40:55,240 --> 00:40:57,870
- Take her. - There you are.

600
00:40:57,870 --> 00:40:59,120
I want to go with you.

601
00:40:59,120 --> 00:41:01,200
I need to get the old folks on the boats.

602
00:41:01,930 --> 00:41:03,760
I'm right behind you. I promise.

603
00:41:07,940 --> 00:41:09,020
Go on.

604
00:41:12,320 --> 00:41:13,080
Another one.

605
00:41:13,080 --> 00:41:15,370
Keep moving. We'll meet you out there.

606
00:41:18,960 --> 00:41:21,230
Won't be able to take that on the boat. It's too big.

607
00:41:21,610 --> 00:41:25,050
- That's the last one! - Three more! Move on!

608
00:41:25,280 --> 00:41:27,140
There's more dragonglass everywhere.

609
00:41:34,850 --> 00:41:36,750
I've got some more daggers over here.

610
00:41:37,750 --> 00:41:39,230
Put that dagger in his bag.

611
00:42:36,600 --> 00:42:37,830
Steady!

612
00:42:58,370 --> 00:42:59,730
Shut the gates.

613
00:42:59,970 --> 00:43:01,480
Close the gates!

614
00:43:05,930 --> 00:43:07,540
Shut the gates!

615
00:43:12,160 --> 00:43:13,400
Wait!

616
00:43:13,490 --> 00:43:16,180
- Open the gate! - Let us in!

617
00:43:17,530 --> 00:43:18,660
Help us!

618
00:43:29,650 --> 00:43:31,030
Please open it!

619
00:43:31,550 --> 00:43:33,090
Open the gate!

620
00:44:19,720 --> 00:44:21,320
Ready your arrows!

621
00:44:40,670 --> 00:44:44,260
- Wait! Wait! - Keep in line! Keep in line!

622
00:44:44,600 --> 00:44:45,990
Get in line!

623
00:44:47,990 --> 00:44:49,210
Get in line!

624
00:44:50,600 --> 00:44:51,860
Get to the front!

625
00:45:49,150 --> 00:45:50,800
Go on, shoot it!

626
00:45:57,920 --> 00:46:01,440
- Wait, wait! - Duncan, hold the line! Hold the line!

627
00:46:06,880 --> 00:46:08,250
Lord Commander!

628
00:46:08,330 --> 00:46:10,490
Get them to the ship and come back for me!

629
00:46:10,500 --> 00:46:12,120
- But you'll never make-- - Now!

630
00:46:16,050 --> 00:46:18,130
- Let me on! - Let me on!

631
00:46:18,370 --> 00:46:20,300
- Bloody fucking move! - Let go!

632
00:46:21,450 --> 00:46:23,120
You should be on one of those boats.

633
00:46:23,120 --> 00:46:25,800
So should you. My little girls got on.

634
00:46:26,120 --> 00:46:28,490
They're gonna let them pass the Wall even if you're not there?

635
00:46:28,490 --> 00:46:31,050
You have my word. I've given orders.

636
00:46:31,050 --> 00:46:33,970
Don't think you're gonna be there to enforce those orders.

637
00:46:35,940 --> 00:46:38,330
If they get through, everyone dies.

638
00:46:41,760 --> 00:46:44,750
Night's Watch, with me!

639
00:46:44,860 --> 00:46:46,760
Move! Move!

640
00:46:48,510 --> 00:46:49,590
Move!

641
00:47:55,310 --> 00:47:56,760
No, no!

642
00:48:06,180 --> 00:48:09,630
- Tormund, the sleigh! - Okay, okay!

643
00:48:57,670 --> 00:48:59,050
The dragonglass.

644
00:48:59,330 --> 00:49:00,790
You and me, then!

645
00:49:01,840 --> 00:49:02,780
Go!

646
00:50:13,380 --> 00:50:14,730
Get the glass.

647
00:53:20,720 --> 00:53:21,800
Come on!

648
00:53:23,180 --> 00:53:24,190
The dragonglass.

649
00:53:24,200 --> 00:53:27,100
Fuck the glass! We are gonna die here!

650
00:53:58,340 --> 00:54:00,410
Oh, fuck!

651
00:54:06,840 --> 00:54:08,410
Tormund, run!

652
00:54:17,960 --> 00:54:20,030
Wun Wun, to the sea.

653
00:55:03,260 --> 00:55:04,400
Quickly!

654
00:55:04,710 --> 00:55:07,030
- Faster, faster! - Run, run!

655
00:55:07,030 --> 00:55:08,620
Quickly!

656
00:55:11,400 --> 00:55:13,890
- Quickly, row! Row! - Go, go, come on!

657
00:55:43,190 --> 00:55:45,980
Let's go! Now, now!

658
00:55:50,270 --> 00:55:51,970
Come on, row!

